    "lawCode": "Amd §364-j, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6012\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the social services law, in relation\nto school-based health centers\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo permanently carve-out School-Based Health Centers (SBHC) from the\nState's Medicaid Managed Care (MMC) program.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill adds 2 new paragraphs (w) and (w-1) to\nSubdivision I of Section 364-j of the Social Services law to create\ndefinitions for a \"School-Based Health Center\" and a \"Sponsoring\norganization\" of a School-Based Health Center.\n\nSection 2 provides that the Commissioner of Health shall not require\nthe services provided by SBHCs to be provided through the State's\nMedicaid Managed program. In addition, the Commissioner of of Health\nis required to:\n\n*reimburse SBHCs sponsored by Federally Qualified Health Centers\n(FQHCs) pursuant to federally mandated rate methodologies;\n\n*reimburse SBHCs that are not sponsored by FQHCs or those who do not\nopt for the FQHC rate at APG rates: and\n\n*develop a standard memorandum of understanding to be entered into by\nsponsoring organizations and managed care providers to provide for the\ndelivery of coordinated health care and participation in quality\nimprovement initiatives.\n\nSection 3 provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nSBHCs provide comprehensive physical, chronic, mental and dental\nhealth services to over 230,000 underserved youth in rural, urban and\nsuburban areas of the State, regardless of insurance status or ability\nto pay. They are a proven model for increasing access to health and\nmental health services, reducing ethnic and racial disparities in the\ncommunities they serve, improving school attendance and performance,\nand saving the State money. According to the New York State Department\nof Health (NYS DOH), 31% of students served are black or African\nAmerican and 44% are identified as Hispanic or Latino. Currently 13%\nare uninsured.\n\nSince 1985, School-Based Health Centers have been \"carved-out\" of the\nMedicaid Managed Care program, enabling them to receive reimbursement\ndirectly from NYS DOH on a fee-for-service basis. NYS DOH is planning\nto end the carve-out as of July 1, 2018. Under the \"carve-in\", NYS DOH\nwill disburse payments directly to MMC health plans and SBHCs will be\nrequired to negotiate the terms and conditions of reimbursement with\nthe health plans and receive payment from them. A report by the\nChildren's Defense Fund found that this transition to managed care\nwould result in a loss of $16.2 million in funds to SBHCs.\n\nSchool-Based Health Centers will not be financially viable if funds\nare reduced for the services that they provide. In the SFY 2017-18\nBudget, direct funding for services to children and adolescents was\nreduced by $3.9 million, a 20% cut. This brings the total reduction in\nfunds for SBHCs since 2008 to $7.3 million or nearly 30%.\n\nThis bill will preserve access to physical, mental, chronic, and\ndental health services for underserved children and adolescents in New\nYork State by supporting the continued financial viability of SBHCs.\nIt will enhance the delivery care to over 200,000 children that rely\non SBHCs by authorizing NYS DOH to develop a standard memorandum of\nunderstanding to be entered into by sponsoring organizations and MMC\nplans to provide for the delivery of coordinated care and\nparticipation in quality improvement initiatives.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nPreservation of SBHCs saves the State money because they have a proven\ntrack record of reducing health care costs including unnecessary\nhospitalizations and emergency room visits.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
